    kg:textSpan "when partitioning a large IT graph, although LeapGNN takes approximately 2800 seconds (roughly the time of one 7.9 Model Accuracy So far we have compared LeapGNN with the state-of-the-art systems with accuracy delity. Approximate methods, such training epoch) longer than random partitioning, it still outperforms P 3 by 1.6 on GAT in a typical 200-epoch training scenario, with partitioning time included. Moreover, several recent GNN-tailored partitioning algorithms [ 24 , 25 , 28 , 54 ] have show" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/isca22_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "isca22_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "isca22_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/isca22_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "32, 34]. This paper aims to improve the performance of full-batch GNN training and inference on multi-core SIMD CPUs without sampling. To understand the performance bottlenecks of these workloads, we Algorithm 1: Parallel vectorized aggregation. Algorithm 2: Fused aggregation and update. input : graph = ( , ) , input feature matrix h 1 , reduction operator , feature processor output : aggregation feature matrix a input : graph = ( , ) , input feature matrix h 1 output : aggregation feature matri" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/isca22_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "isca22_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "isca22_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/isca22_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "on three cores P0-P2 can overlap without synchronization. 4.2 Layer Fusion The aggregation phase is irregular and memory-intensive, while the update phase is regular and compute-intensive. Conventionally, a GNN layer first aggregates all vertices, placing little pressure on compute hardware but a lot on the memory hierarchy, and then updates the vertices, flipping the hardware utilization. We propose to fuse the phases to overlap the memory movement with the compute. Algorithm 2 describes the fu" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/isca22_rag__chunk_020> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "isca22_rag::chunk_020" ;
    kg:docId "isca22_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/isca22_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "aggregation-update. The baseline in the aggregation-only case is basic s aggregation phase. The baseline in the fused aggregation-update case is fusion . Table 5 lists the fraction of private cache accesses eliminated by utilizing the DMA in the two scenarios. In aggregation only, on both datasets, the DMA-aggregation reduces the L1 accesses by over 97%. The core only accesses L1 for building the descriptors or checking for completion. DMA-aggregation also reduces the L2 accesses significantly. " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/osdi21-gandhi_rag__chunk_011> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "osdi21-gandhi_rag::chunk_011" ;
    kg:docId "osdi21-gandhi_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/osdi21-gandhi_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "necessary between CPU and GPU. As a result, the computation is stalled during communication unless they are overlapped using pipelining techniques. Note that current GNN frameworks (e.g., DGL) already overlap computation and communicationwhile the CPU is busy creating the computation graph, the GPU is used to execute an already prepared mini batch. In P 3 , we employ a simple pipelining mechanism, inspired by PipeDreams pipeline parallelism [ 49 ]. Due to the approach we take in P 3 to enable pu" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/osdi21-wang-yuke_rag__chunk_008> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "osdi21-wang-yuke_rag::chunk_008" ;
    kg:docId "osdi21-wang-yuke_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/osdi21-wang-yuke_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "when each neighbor has a lightweight scalar attribute. In this case, the benet of loading parallelization would offset the downside of duplicate loading of some shared neighbors. However, in GNN computing where node embedding size is large, this node-centric loading would trigger signicant unnecessary memory access since the cost of duplicate neighbor loading is now dominant and not offset by per-node parallelism For example, aggregation of node a , b , c , d , and e would load the embeddings of" .

